If $f$ is quasi-DM, then the “fibres” $\mathcal{X}_ T$ of $\mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ are quasi-DM. An algebraic stack $\mathcal{X}$ is quasi-DM exactly if there exists a scheme $U$ and a surjective flat morphism $U \to \mathcal{X}$ of finite presentation which is locally quasi-finite, see Theorem <a href="/tag/06MF" data-tag="06MF">101.21.3</a>. Note the similarity to being Deligne-Mumford, which is defined in terms of having an étale covering by a scheme.